; These adapter forms deserve review because they can discard errors carried
; by Result values. The .ok() branch requires a direct call receiver, but RQL
; cannot join that nested call to its proven workspace callee edge. Flatten's
; iterator item type is also unavailable here, so both remain explicit
; structural review prompts rather than typed error-transfer proofs.
(policy
  :schema-version 1
  :id "bifrost.correctness.rust-error-dropped-by-adapter"
  :name "Rust adapters that can erase Result errors"
  :message "Review this adapter for error loss; this call shape does not prove an Err is discarded"
  :severity note
  :description "Reviews a direct call followed by .ok(), filter_map(Result::ok), and flatten() call shapes. Bifrost does not expose a typed iterator-item transfer contract here, so flatten is a call-shape prompt and does not prove the receiver yields Result items. The .ok() receiver must be a direct call expression, but RQL cannot join its nested call to a proven workspace callee edge; that branch is name-based and can include a direct call returning another type. Clippy's result_filter_map covers only the filter-then-unwrap form."
  :help-uri "https://bifrost.brokk.ai/static-analysis-policies/#built-in-code-smell-pack"
  :tags ["correctness" "rust" "result" "iterator"]
  :analysis
    (analysis
      :type match
      :selector
        (rql
          :schema-version 1
          (language rust
            (union
              (call
                :callee (name "ok")
                :receiver (call :callee (name/regex ".+")))
              (call
                :callee (name "filter_map")
                :args [(text/regex "^Result::ok$")])
              (call :callee (name "flatten")))))))
